{"name":"Bungalow Gold","hex":"#ad8047","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bungalow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A single-storey house, typically with rooms all on one level, or sometimes also with upper rooms set into the roof space.","A thatched or tiled one-story house in India surrounded by a wide veranda; a similar house in this style."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bungalow"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bungalow","blocks":[{"text":"A bungalow is a small house or cottage that is typically single- or one-and-a-half-storey. If a smaller upper storey exists, then it is frequently set in the roof and windows that come out from the roof. It may be surrounded by wide verandas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/149MyrtleReedsburgWI.JPG/330px-149MyrtleReedsburgWI.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bungalow","wikidata":"Q850107"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Elevation and floor plan of an Australian bungalow-style residence, 1939.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_con